 ## Overview
-Multi-Mode Rendering with Iso-surfaces and Meshes is a hybrid rendering technique that combines hardware-accelerated ray-triangle intersection for explicit surface geometry (meshes) and implicit isosurfaces with volume ray marching for direct volume rendering (DVR). It solves the problem of efficiently rendering scenes containing both dense volumetric data and sharp surface boundaries by using surface geometry to define integration bounds for the volume data. Unlike pure volume rendering, it leverages separate bounding volume hierarchies (BVHs) for volume and surface data to maximize empty space skipping, and unlike pure mesh rendering, it retains the ability to visualize internal structures. It is distinguished from its siblings by its specific implementation of a two-stage surface hit detection (mesh BVH then iso-surface BVH) followed by volume integration, utilizing RTX hardware acceleration for the surface components.
+Multi-Mode Rendering with Iso-surfaces and Meshes is a rendering technique that combines hardware-accelerated ray-triangle intersection for surface geometry (meshes/iso-surfaces) with volume ray marching, using clipping planes to control the integration range [S1]. It supports volume data, implicit iso-surfaces, and triangle meshes, using separate BVHs and hardware-accelerated intersection tests [S1]. It is distinguished from its siblings by its specific two-stage surface hit detection (mesh BVH then iso-surface BVH) followed by volume integration, utilizing RTX hardware acceleration for the surface components [S1].
 
 ## How it works
